 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Ночевка в подъезде  (Sleeping in the Hallway)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "kostya": "Костя",
  "max": "Макс",
  "vika": "Вика"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Vika has listened to Max, and she tells him that it's too soon to live together. She says goodnight, and Max is left in the hallway. Then his phone rings. The Russian comes first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Поэтому я не буду давить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Пусть всё остаётся как было.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Да, но я...",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мы обязательно будем жить вместе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "А сейчас спокойной ночи.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Переехал жить в подъезд.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Привет, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Что тебе надо?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Макс, возвращайся.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Нам тебя не хватает.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Мы поделим квартиру на троих.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я постараюсь приехать.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "One line at a time, once more.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Поэтому я не буду давить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Therefore I will not pressure you.' 'Press' is the word behind 'blood pressure'. Vika opens with 'therefore' as if she'd been thinking it over.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Пусть всё остаётся как было.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Let everything remain as it was.' Russian says 'let everything stay as was', with no subject for 'was'. It's a very Russian way to ask for no changes.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Да, но я...",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, but I...' Max trails off. The Russian 'no' looks like English 'no' but means 'but', so the line begins 'da, no ya'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мы обязательно будем жить вместе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We will definitely live together.' The future is made of 'will be' plus 'live', as in 'we will be living'. 'Definitely' comes from 'obligatory'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "А сейчас спокойной ночи.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And now good night.' Literally, 'of a calm night', and the 'of' ending shows it's a wish. It works like 'bon appetit' in that way.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Переехал жить в подъезд.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Moved to live in the hallway.' There's no 'I' and no 'am'. The stairwell of a block of flats is 'podyezd'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Привет, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Hi, Max.' 'Privet' is the casual hello, and Kostya says it on the phone. It's what Russians say to friends.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Что тебе надо?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What do you need?' Russian says 'what to you necessary', and it's a rude way to open a call. Max is still hurt.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Макс, возвращайся.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Max, come back.' 'Vozvrashchaysya' has a 'sya', so it's 'return yourself'. Kostya uses the plain order for a friend.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Нам тебя не хватает.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We miss you.' Literally, 'to us you are not enough'. 'Khvatat'' is also the root behind the word for 'grab'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Мы поделим квартиру на троих.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We will divide the apartment among three.' Russians say 'divide on three'. The sharing word is built from 'divide', with 'po' in front.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я постараюсь приехать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I will try to come.' The 'sya' on 'try' gives 'I will try myself'. 'Come' means arriving by vehicle. And the hallway scene, from the top.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Поэтому я не буду давить.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Пусть всё остаётся как было.",
   "gap": 0.5
